1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,760
 Alright, we are through with the admin stuff and we can finally start to talk about some

2
00:00:04,760 --> 00:00:06,560
 of the course content.

3
00:00:06,560 --> 00:00:11,960
 As the name suggests, this course is in two parts, modeling and control.

4
00:00:11,960 --> 00:00:15,240
 And when I say modeling, I'm not talking about the sculpting kind of modeling.

5
00:00:15,240 --> 00:00:19,520
 And I'm not even talking about the computer aided design, CAD kind of modeling.

6
00:00:19,520 --> 00:00:23,360
 I'm talking about mathematical modeling and you may think this is boring but mathematical

7
00:00:23,360 --> 00:00:28,120
 models of the tools that engineers used to make decisions every day and a really fundamental

8
00:00:28,120 --> 00:00:31,960
 level mathematical models tell us how variables in a system are related.

9
00:00:31,960 --> 00:00:34,680
 And you've almost certainly met these before.

10
00:00:34,680 --> 00:00:38,200
 It's pretty hard to get to second or third year of engineering in or having seen a mathematical

11
00:00:38,200 --> 00:00:41,040
 model unless you're a civil engineer.

12
00:00:41,040 --> 00:00:45,480
 Typically, we use mathematical models to work at how some variables, which we call

13
00:00:45,480 --> 00:00:48,880
 inputs, affect other variables, which we call outputs.

14
00:00:48,880 --> 00:00:52,840
 Generally speaking, inputs of variables which drive changes to a system.

15
00:00:52,840 --> 00:00:55,240
 There are some types of inputs we can control.

16
00:00:55,240 --> 00:00:59,360
 For example, in a model of a car, a controllable input would be something like the amount

17
00:00:59,360 --> 00:01:01,800
 you've pressed down on the throttle pedal.

18
00:01:01,800 --> 00:01:06,520
 And some inputs we can't control, things like again in the model of a car, maybe the

19
00:01:06,520 --> 00:01:09,600
 aerodynamic forces or bumps in the road.

20
00:01:09,600 --> 00:01:13,080
 These uncontrollable inputs are sometimes called disturbances.

21
00:01:13,080 --> 00:01:18,320
 There are a subset of inputs that kind of characterize the system but don't necessarily

22
00:01:18,320 --> 00:01:20,480
 drive changes to it.

23
00:01:20,480 --> 00:01:25,720
 These things don't usually change with time and we call them the parameters of the system.

24
00:01:25,720 --> 00:01:31,120
 Things, for example, like the mass of a car, or the stiffness of the springs on the car, or

25
00:01:31,120 --> 00:01:33,320
 the stickiness of the tires or the air pressure.

26
00:01:33,320 --> 00:01:37,920
 We typically regard parameters as intrinsic inputs and we don't list them as such when we

27
00:01:37,920 --> 00:01:41,880
 body mathematical models, but it's important to note that every bit of information that

28
00:01:41,880 --> 00:01:45,200
 you give your model is in some way an input to that model.

29
00:01:45,200 --> 00:01:49,160
 Outputs simply said are variables that you're interested in and what you decide to call

30
00:01:49,160 --> 00:01:52,640
 an output will probably depend on the reason that you're building a model.

31
00:01:52,640 --> 00:01:55,280
 Outputs are usually things we don't have control over.

32
00:01:55,280 --> 00:01:59,720
 In other words, we don't usually take inputs as outputs to our models.

33
00:01:59,720 --> 00:02:01,760
 Otherwise they'd be pretty boring.

34
00:02:01,760 --> 00:02:05,000
 Most of the time you'll know what an output is because it's the reason you've decided

35
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:07,120
 to build the model in the first place.

36
00:02:07,120 --> 00:02:09,360
 It's the answer to the question that you're trying to solve.

37
00:02:09,360 --> 00:02:12,920
 Let's take a look at problem 1.1 and see if we can identify which of the inputs and which

38
00:02:12,920 --> 00:02:14,840
 of the outputs based on our intuition.

39
00:02:14,840 --> 00:02:17,840
 And these are the problems you've probably seen before in your prior courses.
